from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: how much do you already know?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The international body that unites French-speaking countries and promotes the language is called…","options":["the European Union","la Francophonie","the Louvre Accord","the Schengen Zone"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Organisation internationale de la Francophonie links dozens of states and governments where French is spoken — see ''The French language and la Francophonie''."},{"prompt":"The French Revolution began in…","options":["1715","1789","1848","1914"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The storming of the Bastille on 14 July 1789 is the Revolution''s traditional starting point — covered in ''A thousand years of France''."},{"prompt":"Which painter is most associated with Impressionism and his garden water lilies at Giverny?","options":["Henri Matisse","Claude Monet","Paul Cezanne","Eugene Delacroix"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Monet''s series paintings, including the water lilies, define Impressionism''s interest in light."},{"prompt":"The author of the novel ''Les Miserables'' is…","options":["Gustave Flaubert","Marcel Proust","Victor Hugo","Albert Camus"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Victor Hugo wrote ''Les Miserables'' and ''The Hunchback of Notre-Dame''."},{"prompt":"French ''laicite'' refers to…","options":["a cooking technique","the separation of religion from the state","a style of Gothic architecture","a wine classification"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Laicite is the principle of state secularism, rooted in the 1905 law — see ''Laicite and French values''."},{"prompt":"The film movement known as the ''New Wave'' (Nouvelle Vague) emerged in France during the…","options":["1890s","1920s","late 1950s and 1960s","1990s"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Directors such as Godard and Truffaut launched the New Wave at the end of the 1950s."},{"prompt":"Gothic cathedrals such as Chartres and Notre-Dame de Paris are known for…","options":["flat wooden ceilings","pointed arches and large stained-glass windows","domes copied from Rome","a complete absence of decoration"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Gothic style used pointed arches, ribbed vaults, and flying buttresses to raise walls of stained glass."},{"prompt":"Which of these is a classic dish of French gastronomy?","options":["sushi","coq au vin","pad thai","goulash"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Coq au vin, chicken braised in wine, is a staple of French regional cooking — see ''Cuisine, fashion, and modern France''."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'France and the Francophone world', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"After this lesson you will be able to locate France within Europe, describe its main regions, and explain the difference between France the country and the broader Francophone world. You will be able to name several continents where French is an everyday language and say why. Warm-up, drawing on the diagnostic: the organisation that links French-speaking states is called what? Keep that word in mind, because it is the thread that ties this whole course together. France is only the starting point; the story quickly becomes global, reaching across Africa, the Americas, and the Pacific. By the end you should be comfortable saying that ''French culture'' is not confined to one hexagon-shaped country but is shared, reshaped, and argued over by hundreds of millions of people who were never ruled from Versailles and never will be."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"France sits in Western Europe, bordered by the Atlantic, the Mediterranean, and neighbours including Spain, Italy, Germany, and Belgium. Its mainland is often called the Hexagon for its rough six-sided shape, and it is divided into administrative regions, each with its own food, dialect, and landscape, from Brittany on the windy Atlantic coast to sunlit Provence. But France is also an overseas country, governing territories in the Caribbean, the Indian Ocean, South America, and the Pacific. Beyond France itself lies the Francophone world: the many places where French is a first or common language, including large parts of West and Central Africa, Quebec in Canada, Belgium, Switzerland, and small communities worldwide. French spread through medieval prestige, later empire, trade, and schooling. Today more people speak French outside France than inside it, which means French culture is genuinely plural, carrying accents and ideas from Dakar, Montreal, and Brussels as much as from Paris."},{"heading":"Places and voices to know","text":"A few anchors help. Paris, the capital, has long been a magnet for writers and artists, but it is not the whole country. Lyon is famous for food, Marseille for its Mediterranean port and mix of cultures, and Bordeaux for wine. Abroad, Quebec preserves a distinct French identity in North America, with its own music and expressions. In Senegal, French works alongside Wolof and other languages, and writers such as Leopold Sedar Senghor shaped a proud Francophone literature. Belgium and Switzerland each have French-speaking regions with their own traditions. The Caribbean islands of Guadeloupe and Martinique blend French with Creole. Recognising these voices matters because the rest of the course, on literature, music, and values, makes more sense once you see France as a hub in a wide network rather than an isolated nation. The map is bigger than you might expect, and far more interesting for it."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Try these, then check yourself. 1) What nickname describes mainland France''s shape? 2) Name one French-speaking region of Canada. 3) Name one African country where French is widely spoken. 4) Are there more French speakers inside France or outside it? 5) Name a French city known especially for food. Answers: 1) the Hexagon. 2) Quebec. 3) Senegal (also Ivory Coast, Mali, Democratic Republic of the Congo, and many others). 4) Outside France, mainly because of large populations in Africa. 5) Lyon, though Paris, Marseille, and many towns would all be defensible answers. If you missed any, reread ''The idea'', which sets out the core geography that the later cultural lessons quietly assume throughout."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"A common mistake is to treat French culture as a single Parisian thing, when it is really a web of regional and global variants that borrow from one another constantly. Another is to assume every Francophone place was a colony; some, like Belgium and Switzerland, simply have French-speaking populations of their own. A third slip is confusing France the state with the Francophone world, a much larger idea. Exit ticket, answer before moving on: (a) In one sentence, what is the difference between France and the Francophone world? (b) Name two continents besides Europe where French is widely spoken. Answers: (a) France is a single country, while the Francophone world is every place where French is a first or common language, spanning several continents. (b) Africa and North America, with strong claims also for the Caribbean, South America via French Guiana, and the Pacific."}]}'::jsonb, 'A ''Francophone'' country is one where French is widely spoken; it does not have to be in Europe or ruled from Paris.', 12),
  (3, 'A thousand years of France: from the Middle Ages to the Republics', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"This lesson gives you a timeline you can hang everything else on. By the end you will be able to place the big eras of French history in order: medieval France, the Ancien Regime, the Enlightenment, the Revolution and Napoleon, and the long sequence of Republics. You will be able to explain, in plain terms, why 1789 is the hinge of the whole story and what people mean when they say France is on its Fifth Republic. Warm-up from the diagnostic: in what year did the French Revolution begin? Hold that date, because almost everything in modern France is dated as before or after it. History here is not trivia; it is the key that unlocks the art, the values, and even the arguments about religion you will meet later in the course."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Medieval France grew out of the lands of the Franks, slowly pulled together by kings around Paris while great cathedrals rose across the country. The Ancien Regime, the old order before 1789, was an absolute monarchy in which the king, most famously Louis XIV at Versailles, claimed near-total power, and society was split into rigid estates of clergy, nobility, and everyone else. In the 1700s the Enlightenment challenged that order with reason: thinkers like Voltaire, Rousseau, and Montesquieu argued for liberty, tolerance, and limits on power. Their ideas helped ignite the French Revolution of 1789, which toppled the monarchy, proclaimed the rights of man, and led to turmoil, then to Napoleon Bonaparte, who built an empire and spread French law across Europe. The nineteenth and twentieth centuries swung between monarchy, empire, and republic until the Republic took firm hold. France is now in its Fifth Republic, founded in 1958."},{"heading":"Turning points and figures","text":"A handful of names and moments do a lot of work. Louis XIV, the Sun King, embodies the Ancien Regime and built the palace of Versailles as a stage for royal power. The Enlightenment philosophers, especially Voltaire and Rousseau, supplied the vocabulary of rights and reason. The storming of the Bastille on 14 July 1789 became the Revolution''s symbol and is now France''s national day. The Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen set out ideals of liberty and equality. Napoleon Bonaparte crowned himself emperor, reorganised the state, and left a legal code still influential today. Across the 1800s France cycled through restored kings, a Second Empire under Napoleon III, and finally durable republics. Marianne, the female figure of the Republic, and the motto ''Liberty, Equality, Fraternity'' date from this struggle and still appear on buildings and coins across the country today."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Order and explain, then check. 1) Put these in time order: Napoleon''s empire, the Ancien Regime, the Fifth Republic, the Enlightenment. 2) What does 14 July commemorate? 3) Name one Enlightenment thinker. 4) Which republic is France in now? 5) Who was the Sun King? Answers: 1) the Ancien Regime, then the Enlightenment, then Napoleon''s empire, then the Fifth Republic. 2) the storming of the Bastille in 1789, now the national day. 3) Voltaire (also Rousseau or Montesquieu). 4) the Fifth Republic, founded in 1958. 5) Louis XIV, who built Versailles and personified absolute monarchy. If the ordering tripped you up, redraw the timeline from ''The idea'' on paper; a visible line fixes the sequence far better than rereading does."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"People often blur the Revolution and Napoleon into one event, but the Revolution came first and Napoleon rose from its chaos to build an empire. Another mistake is to think France has had one unbroken Republic; in fact it has had five, with monarchies and empires in between. A third is to date the Enlightenment after the Revolution; the ideas came first and helped cause it. Exit ticket: (a) Why is 1789 considered the hinge of modern French history? (b) What is the difference between the Ancien Regime and the Republic? Answers: (a) 1789 ended absolute monarchy and launched the ideals of rights, liberty, and equality that still define French public life. (b) The Ancien Regime was an absolute monarchy divided into estates, while the Republic is based on citizenship and popular sovereignty, not inherited royal rule."}]}'::jsonb, 'The French Republics are numbered: the First Republic followed the 1789 Revolution, and France today lives under the Fifth Republic, founded in 1958.', 15),
  (4, 'The French language and la Francophonie', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to say where the French language comes from, how it became a world language, and what institutions try to shape it today. You will be able to explain la Francophonie, the global community of French speakers, and give examples of how French varies from place to place. Warm-up from the diagnostic: what is the name of the body that unites French-speaking states? French is more than grammar and vocabulary; for centuries it was the language of diplomacy, courts, and learning, and that prestige still echoes. Understanding the language also helps you read the later lessons, because literature, song, and even political slogans all depend on the particular music and precision that speakers of French prize so highly in their tongue."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"French is a Romance language, grown from the Latin that Roman rule left in Gaul and reshaped over centuries into the speech of medieval France. Paris''s dialect gradually became the standard, and royal power spread it. By the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries, French was the common language of European diplomacy and the educated elite, admired for clarity and elegance. The Academie francaise, founded in 1635, still issues opinions on correct usage, though living speech ignores it as often as not. Through exploration, trade, and empire, French travelled far beyond Europe, taking root in Africa, the Americas, and the Pacific. Today la Francophonie names both the fact of sharing the language and the organisation that promotes it and cooperates on culture and education. The language is not frozen: Quebecois, Belgian, Senegalese, and Caribbean French each have their own words, sounds, and rhythms, and new slang constantly bubbles up from the streets of every French-speaking city."},{"heading":"Institutions and varieties","text":"Several landmarks are worth remembering. The Academie francaise is the famous forty-member body that compiles a dictionary and comments on usage, a symbol of the French care for language even when people cheerfully break its rules. The Organisation internationale de la Francophonie is the modern political and cultural network of French-speaking states and governments. Regional varieties give the language its real life: in Quebec you might hear ''char'' for car; West African French carries its own vocabulary and cadence; Belgian French uses ''septante'' for seventy where France says ''soixante-dix''. Verlan, a playful slang that flips syllables, thrives among young people in France. None of this makes one version more correct than another; it shows a living language doing what living languages do. When the course later reaches literature and song, keep this variety in mind, because many celebrated voices write in a French shaped far from Paris and proud of it."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Answer, then check. 1) From which ancient language does French descend? 2) What is the Academie francaise? 3) What does la Francophonie refer to? 4) Give one example of regional variation in French. 5) True or false: there is only one correct way to speak French. Answers: 1) Latin; French is a Romance language. 2) A body founded in 1635 that advises on correct French usage and compiles a dictionary. 3) Both the shared fact of speaking French worldwide and the organisation that promotes it. 4) Quebec''s ''char'' for car, or Belgium''s ''septante'' for seventy, among many. 5) False; standard French coexists with many respected regional varieties. If question five felt tricky, revisit ''Institutions and varieties'', which is the heart of this lesson and its most important single point by far."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"A frequent error is to assume the Academie francaise controls how people actually speak; it advises, but everyday French changes with or without approval. Another is to think French belongs only to France, when hundreds of millions speak it elsewhere and shape it daily. A third is to confuse la Francophonie the community with any single government. Exit ticket: (a) Why is French called a Romance language? (b) In one sentence, what is la Francophonie? Answers: (a) Because it descends from Latin, the language of Rome, like Spanish and Italian. (b) La Francophonie is the worldwide community of French speakers, and the organisation that promotes the language and cooperation among the states and governments that use it, far beyond the borders of France itself."}]}'::jsonb, 'French is a Romance language, descended from Latin, like Spanish, Italian, Portuguese, and Romanian.', 13),
  (5, 'Checkpoint 1', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Mainland France is sometimes nicknamed the Hexagon because of its…","options":["six former colonies","roughly six-sided shape","six official languages","six large rivers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The nickname comes from the country''s rough six-sided outline."},{"prompt":"The Ancien Regime refers to…","options":["the government after 1789","the old absolute monarchy before the Revolution","Napoleon''s empire","the Fifth Republic"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Ancien Regime was the old order of absolute monarchy and estates before 1789."},{"prompt":"Which thinkers are associated with the French Enlightenment?","options":["Monet and Renoir","Voltaire and Rousseau","Hugo and Flaubert","Godard and Truffaut"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Voltaire, Rousseau, and Montesquieu were leading Enlightenment philosophers."},{"prompt":"France today lives under its…","options":["First Republic","Third Republic","Fifth Republic","Second Empire"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The Fifth Republic was founded in 1958 and continues today."},{"prompt":"The French language descends from…","options":["Greek","Latin","Old Norse","Arabic"],"answer":1,"explanation":"French is a Romance language, grown from Latin."},{"prompt":"La Francophonie is best described as…","options":["a wine region","the worldwide community of French speakers","a type of cathedral","a film festival"],"answer":1,"explanation":"It is the global French-speaking community and the organisation that promotes the language."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (6, 'French literature: from Moliere to Camus', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"This lesson introduces five towering French writers and the kinds of books they wrote, so that a reference to any of them in conversation or reading will no longer be a mystery. By the end you will be able to match Moliere, Hugo, Flaubert, Proust, and Camus to their era, their best-known works, and the idea each is remembered for. Warm-up from the diagnostic: who wrote ''Les Miserables''? French literature is one of the richest in the world, and it carries the arguments of French culture, about love, justice, class, time, and the meaning of life, into stories people still read everywhere. You do not need to have read these books to understand why they matter; this lesson gives you the map, and you can explore the territory later at your own pace."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"French literature spans centuries and many moods. In the 1600s, the age of classicism, Moliere wrote sharp comedies that mocked hypocrisy and pretension, and his plays are still performed constantly. The 1800s brought Romanticism and then realism. Victor Hugo, a Romantic giant, wrote sweeping novels of justice and mercy such as ''Les Miserables'' and ''The Hunchback of Notre-Dame''. Gustave Flaubert pursued realism and a near-perfect prose style in ''Madame Bovary'', a careful study of a disappointed provincial woman. In the twentieth century, Marcel Proust wrote ''In Search of Lost Time'', an enormous novel about memory set off by the taste of a small cake. Albert Camus, writing during and after the Second World War, explored the absurd and the ethics of revolt in ''The Stranger'' and ''The Plague''. Together they show literature moving from the stage to the inner life."},{"heading":"Key writers and works","text":"Fix each author to one anchor. Moliere equals comedy and the theatre, puncturing frauds and snobs; think of ''Tartuffe''. Victor Hugo equals the grand Romantic novel of social conscience; think of Jean Valjean in ''Les Miserables''. Gustave Flaubert equals realism and painstaking style; think of Emma Bovary, trapped by her own daydreams. Marcel Proust equals memory and time; think of the narrator tasting a madeleine and recovering a whole lost childhood. Albert Camus equals the absurd and moral courage; think of a man indifferent to the world in ''The Stranger'', or a town fighting an epidemic in ''The Plague''. These five do not cover everything; poets, playwrights, and modern novelists abound. But learn these anchors and you will have a sturdy frame on which to hang any further reading in French literature you choose to do, whether in translation or, one day, in the original French itself."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Match and recall, then check. 1) Who wrote comedies mocking hypocrites in the 1600s? 2) Which author wrote ''Madame Bovary''? 3) What single object famously triggers memory in Proust''s great novel? 4) Name a novel by Albert Camus. 5) Which writer created Jean Valjean? Answers: 1) Moliere, master of classical comedy. 2) Gustave Flaubert, a founder of literary realism. 3) a madeleine, a small cake, dipped in tea. 4) ''The Stranger'' or ''The Plague''. 5) Victor Hugo, in ''Les Miserables''. If any slipped, reread ''Key writers and works'', where each author is pinned to a single memorable anchor; those anchors are designed to stick, so a second pass should lock them firmly into place for good."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"A common mix-up is to swap Hugo and Flaubert; remember Hugo for sweeping Romantic drama and social justice, Flaubert for cool, exact realism. Another is to call Camus a nineteenth-century writer; he belongs to the twentieth, shaped by war. A third is to think Proust''s novel is short; it is famously vast, one of the longest in literature. Exit ticket: (a) Name the movement, Romanticism or realism, that best fits Flaubert. (b) Which writer is linked with the idea of the absurd? Answers: (a) realism, since Flaubert aimed at precise, unsentimental observation of ordinary life. (b) Albert Camus, who explored the absurd gap between our search for meaning and a silent universe, and argued for revolt and solidarity in response rather than despair."}]}'::jsonb, 'French writers are often grouped by movement: classicism, Romanticism, realism, and the twentieth-century novel each have their own aims and style.', 14),
  (7, 'Art, music, and cinema: Gothic to the New Wave', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"This lesson covers French achievement in the visual arts, music, and film, so you can recognise the great movements and name their leading figures. By the end you will be able to describe the Gothic cathedral, explain what Impressionism tried to capture, name key painters, and say what the New Wave did to cinema. Warm-up from the diagnostic: which painter is famous for water lilies at Giverny? France has been a crossroads of art for a thousand years, from the master builders of medieval cathedrals to the filmmakers who reinvented how movies are made. You will not become an art historian in one lesson, but you will gain the vocabulary and the names that let you walk into a museum, or a conversation, and feel that you belong there."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"French art stretches from stone to celluloid. In the Middle Ages, builders raised Gothic cathedrals such as Chartres and Notre-Dame de Paris, using pointed arches, ribbed vaults, and flying buttresses to open walls into vast sheets of coloured stained glass. Centuries later, in the 1870s, the Impressionists broke with academic painting to capture light and fleeting moments outdoors; Claude Monet and Pierre-Auguste Renoir led the way with loose, luminous brushwork. Paul Cezanne then pushed toward structure and form, paving the road to modern art, and in the twentieth century Henri Matisse exploded with bold, flat colour. French music ranges from Debussy''s shimmering compositions to the beloved chanson tradition of singers like Edith Piaf. In cinema, France both invented the medium, with the Lumiere brothers, and renewed it: the New Wave of the late 1950s, led by Godard and Truffaut, made personal, inventive films that changed world cinema."},{"heading":"Movements and makers","text":"Pin down a few anchors. Gothic equals height and light: Chartres and Notre-Dame, walls of stained glass held up by flying buttresses. Impressionism equals capturing light and the passing moment: Monet''s water lilies and haystacks, Renoir''s sunny crowds. Cezanne equals turning nature into solid form, the bridge to Cubism. Matisse equals joyous, daring colour and later cut-paper collages. In music, Edith Piaf equals the emotional chanson, and Claude Debussy equals delicate, atmospheric sound. In film, the Lumiere brothers equal the very birth of cinema in the 1890s, while the New Wave, with Jean-Luc Godard and Francois Truffaut, equals young directors shooting on real streets, breaking rules, and treating the director as an author. These anchors cover a lot of ground. Carry them into any gallery or film club and you will find you can follow, and join, the conversation with real confidence and curiosity from the very first minute."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Recall and match, then check. 1) Name a feature of Gothic cathedral architecture. 2) Which two painters are core Impressionists named in this lesson? 3) Which French painter bridged toward modern art with his focus on form? 4) What was the New Wave? 5) Name a famous French chanson singer. Answers: 1) pointed arches, ribbed vaults, flying buttresses, or large stained-glass windows. 2) Claude Monet and Pierre-Auguste Renoir. 3) Paul Cezanne. 4) a film movement of the late 1950s and 1960s, led by Godard and Truffaut, that renewed cinema with personal, inventive filmmaking. 5) Edith Piaf. If any answer was shaky, reread ''Movements and makers'', which links every name to a short, vivid anchor built precisely so that these artists and their ideas stay with you long afterward."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"One slip is to call every old French church Gothic; the Gothic style is specific, with pointed arches and soaring glass, and earlier churches are Romanesque. Another is to treat Cezanne as an Impressionist; he started near them but moved toward form and is better seen as a bridge to modern art. A third is to date the New Wave too early; it belongs to the late 1950s and 1960s. Exit ticket: (a) What did the Impressionists mainly try to capture? (b) Who are two leading New Wave directors? Answers: (a) light and fleeting, momentary effects, often painted quickly and outdoors to catch a particular time of day. (b) Jean-Luc Godard and Francois Truffaut, among others, who put the director''s personal vision at the centre of filmmaking and inspired movements well beyond France."}]}'::jsonb, 'Impressionism gets its name from Monet''s painting ''Impression, Sunrise''; it was first used as an insult by a critic.', 14),
  (8, 'Key terms of French culture', 'vocab', '{"items":[{"term":"la Francophonie","translation":"the worldwide community of French speakers, and the organisation that promotes the language","example":"Senegal and Quebec are both part of la Francophonie, though they are far from France."},{"term":"Ancien Regime","translation":"the ''old order'' of absolute monarchy and social estates in France before the 1789 Revolution","example":"Under the Ancien Regime, Louis XIV ruled from Versailles with near-total power."},{"term":"laicite","translation":"the French principle that the state is secular and stays separate from religion","example":"Laicite is why French public schools avoid promoting any particular faith."},{"term":"Impressionism","translation":"a painting movement of the 1870s that aimed to capture light and fleeting moments","example":"Monet''s loose, bright brushwork is a textbook example of Impressionism."},{"term":"Nouvelle Vague","translation":"the ''New Wave'', a French film movement of the late 1950s and 1960s that renewed cinema","example":"The Nouvelle Vague encouraged directors to shoot on real streets and break old rules."},{"term":"gastronomy","translation":"the art and culture of fine eating, a field where France is world-renowned","example":"French gastronomy, from baguettes to coq au vin, is listed by UNESCO as cultural heritage."},{"term":"chanson","translation":"a tradition of French song that prizes lyrics and emotion, as sung by Edith Piaf","example":"A classic chanson can tell a whole life story in three verses."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (9, 'Cuisine, fashion, laicite, and modern France', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"This final reading pulls together everyday culture and civic values, so you can speak about French food, fashion, secularism, and France''s place in the world today. By the end you will be able to describe what makes French gastronomy special, name France''s role in global fashion and design, explain laicite and the national motto, and say why France still carries outsized cultural weight. Warm-up from the diagnostic: what does laicite mean? These topics may feel lighter than revolutions and great novels, but they are where culture is lived day to day, at the table, in the street, and in the rules a society sets for itself. Understanding them rounds out your picture of France as a living, modern country, not just a museum of past glories, however magnificent those glories undeniably are."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"French cuisine is famous worldwide for both humble staples and refined cooking: fresh bread, cheese, and wine alongside dishes like coq au vin and elaborate restaurant menus. The shared, unhurried meal is so valued that UNESCO recognised the ''gastronomic meal of the French'' as cultural heritage. In fashion and design, Paris has long been a capital of haute couture, with houses such as Chanel and Dior shaping global style, and the city hosts major fashion shows. In public life, laicite, the separation of religion and state rooted in a 1905 law, shapes schools and government, aiming to keep public institutions neutral. The Republic''s motto, ''Liberty, Equality, Fraternity'', and the figure of Marianne express its ideals. Modern France remains a cultural power far beyond its size: its language, cinema, cuisine, philosophy, and fashion influence the whole world, and it works hard, through la Francophonie and its arts, to keep that influence alive."},{"heading":"Everyday culture and civic values","text":"A few anchors tie it together. Gastronomy equals the shared meal as ritual: good ingredients, several courses, time with others, honoured enough to earn UNESCO status. Fashion equals Paris as a style capital, home to houses like Chanel and Dior and to seasonal shows watched worldwide. Laicite equals a secular state that keeps religion out of public institutions, a principle that sometimes sparks heated debate about dress and identity. The motto ''Liberty, Equality, Fraternity'' equals the Republic''s promise to its citizens, inherited from the Revolution you studied earlier. Modern France equals soft power: a mid-sized country whose culture reaches far beyond its borders through film, food, ideas, and the French language itself. Notice how the earlier lessons feed this one; the Revolution gave the motto, the language lesson gave la Francophonie, and the art lesson gave the cinema that still carries French culture to screens across the entire world today."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Answer, then check. 1) What honour did UNESCO give the French meal? 2) Name a famous French fashion house. 3) In one phrase, what is laicite? 4) What is the motto of the French Republic? 5) Give one way modern France projects cultural influence abroad. Answers: 1) recognition as intangible cultural heritage, the ''gastronomic meal of the French''. 2) Chanel or Dior, among others. 3) the separation of religion and the state, keeping public institutions secular. 4) ''Liberty, Equality, Fraternity''. 5) through cinema, cuisine, fashion, philosophy, or the French language and la Francophonie. If any answer was unsure, revisit ''Everyday culture and civic values'', which gathers the whole lesson into five tidy anchors meant to be easy to carry away with you after the course ends."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"One mistake is to reduce French food to fancy restaurants; just as important is the everyday ritual of a shared, leisurely meal. Another is to confuse laicite with hostility to religion; it is meant as neutrality of the state, not suppression of belief, though its application is debated. A third is to assume a country''s cultural power matches its size; France shows that influence can far outrun population. Exit ticket: (a) Why is the French meal considered cultural heritage? (b) What is the aim of laicite? Answers: (a) because the shared, multi-course meal is valued as a social ritual that binds people together, not merely as food. (b) to keep the state and its public institutions religiously neutral, so that citizens of every faith, and none, are treated equally in the public sphere."}]}'::jsonb, 'French gastronomy was inscribed on UNESCO''s list of intangible cultural heritage in 2010, a recognition of the shared meal as a social ritual.', 14),
  (10, 'Checkpoint 2', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Victor Hugo is best remembered as the author of…","options":["''Madame Bovary''","''Les Miserables''","''The Stranger''","''In Search of Lost Time''"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Hugo wrote ''Les Miserables'' and ''The Hunchback of Notre-Dame''."},{"prompt":"Which writer is linked with the idea of the absurd?","options":["Moliere","Gustave Flaubert","Albert Camus","Marcel Proust"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Camus explored the absurd in ''The Stranger'' and ''The Plague''."},{"prompt":"Impressionism is most associated with which painter?","options":["Henri Matisse","Claude Monet","Moliere","Francois Truffaut"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Monet, with Renoir, led the Impressionist movement of the 1870s."},{"prompt":"The French principle keeping the state separate from religion is called…","options":["Francophonie","laicite","chanson","gastronomy"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Laicite is the principle of state secularism, rooted in the 1905 law."},{"prompt":"The ''gastronomic meal of the French'' was recognised by UNESCO as…","options":["a world sport","intangible cultural heritage","a type of architecture","an endangered language"],"answer":1,"explanation":"UNESCO listed the shared French meal as intangible cultural heritage in 2010."},{"prompt":"The motto of the French Republic is…","options":["''Order and Progress''","''Liberty, Equality, Fraternity''","''In God We Trust''","''Unity in Diversity''"],"answer":1,"explanation":"''Liberte, Egalite, Fraternite'' dates from the Revolution and still defines the Republic."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
